          "session_id": "full-g-md-017",
          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My blood sugar has been over 20 all day and I feel sick. How urgent is that, and what should I be told about diabetic emergencies?",
          "answer": "**Get medical help now.** Blood sugar over 20 with nausea is a combination the guidance treats as a possible emergency. Call your local emergency number, or go to the nearest emergency department, rather than waiting to see if it passes. Don't drive yourself. One source suggested a doctor or urgent care service, but the more urgent advice is the one to follow.\n\nYour nausea already matches one of the signs that call for emergency services immediately. The guidance lists these: nausea, vomiting, or stomach pain; deep, rapid, or laboured breathing, or breath that smells fruity or like nail-polish remover; drowsiness, confusion, or difficulty staying awake; severe dehydration, a very dry mouth, or not passing urine; and being unable to keep fluids down. These signs can point to diabetic ketoacidosis or a hyperosmolar hyperglycaemic state. Both can develop over hours to days and are life-threatening (source: *Diabetic emergencies: very high and very low blood sugar*). If you notice any of the other signs, tell the emergency service straight away.\n\nA triage tool I checked returned \"routine\" because none of its fixed rules matched your wording. That result doesn't reflect the emergency guidance, which names nausea with very high glucose as an emergency, so please don't rely on it. If you already have a sick-day plan from your clinician, follow it, but still call for help given these signs. Illness raises blood sugar, and medication should not simply be stopped because someone is not eating (source: *Sticking with a diabetes regimen*). I can't tell you what to take or how much insulin or other medication to use. Your care team or the emergency service can advise you on that.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
          "wall_ms": 15145.61422099996,
